<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!--
      The app's first permission, and it arrives with the check that needs it rather than ahead of it —
      android-standards.md requires that, and it is also the only way a buyer standing in a shop will
      ever grant it. It is requested on the microphone screen with the reason on screen first, and every
      check reports UNKNOWN rather than a fault if it is refused.

      Used for two measurements and nothing else: three seconds to measure the microphone, and about a
      second and a half to listen for a test tone from the loudspeaker. The samples are analysed in
      memory and discarded. Nothing is written to storage and there is no network code in this app.

      Deliberately NOT requested: READ_PHONE_STATE. The IMEI has been privileged since Android 10 on
      every device regardless of target SDK, so asking would gain nothing and look alarming.
    -->
    <!--
      The first network permission this app has ever asked for, and worth being precise about why.

      Until now nothing left the device at all: every check reads sensors, every report is written to local
      storage, and the Wi-Fi checks only ever read connection *state*. That is why the privacy line on Home
      could be written as plainly as it is.

      It is needed now for exactly two requests, both to the licence server, and both only when a buyer
      chooses to redeem a pass code:

        POST /redeem   a code, and a hash of this device id salted with that code
        POST /issue    a Play purchase receipt, to turn it into a code

      Nothing else uses it. No analytics, no crash reporting, no advertising — none of those exist in this
      project. The device hash is salted with the code precisely so the server cannot recognise a handset
      across two codes; see DeviceFingerprint for why that is not negotiable.

      **This changes the Play Data safety declaration.** It was truthfully "no data collected" while the app
      could not reach a network. Once a redeem is possible, the form has to say so.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECORD_AUDIO" />

    <!--
      The camera, on the same terms: requested on the screen that opens it, with the reason first, and
      never on launch.

      Each camera is opened for about a second, a few frames are read into memory to measure whether the
      sensor is imaging, and they are discarded. No photograph is taken and nothing is written to storage.

      Not needed for the flashlight: CameraManager.setTorchMode requires no permission, so the torch test
      asks for nothing. The obvious implementation — open the camera and set FLASH_MODE_TORCH — would have
      dragged this permission into a test that does not need it.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.CAMERA" />

    <!--
      The vibration test. An install-time permission: granted on install, never prompted for, and not
      treated as sensitive by Play.

      This was missing until a real phone caught it, and the way it failed is worth recording. Reading
      the motor's capabilities needs no permission, so hasVibrator() and hasAmplitudeControl() both
      answered normally; only vibrate() threw SecurityException. The app reported "the phone would not
      let the app run the motor. Check Do Not Disturb is off" — blaming a working handset for a line
      missing from this file. VibrationDriver now separates a refused permission from a declined
      request precisely so that class of mistake cannot be made silently again.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.VIBRATE" />

    <!--
      The radio test. All three of these are install-time permissions: they are granted when the app is
      installed, the buyer is never prompted, and Play does not treat any of them as sensitive.

      ACCESS_WIFI_STATE reads whether Wi-Fi is switched on. ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE reads whether the live
      network is Wi-Fi and whether traffic is actually passing, which is how the radio is proved: a phone
      that has joined a network has already transmitted, received and negotiated an address.

      Deliberately NOT requested: ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION. Scanning for nearby networks is the obvious way
      to test a Wi-Fi radio and it costs a location permission, because Android treats a list of nearby
      networks as a location — correctly. Association proves more and costs nothing, so there is no scan
      anywhere in this app. Also not requested: BLUETOOTH_CONNECT and BLUETOOTH_SCAN, which would be
      needed to pair or discover devices, and CHANGE_WIFI_STATE, which has been unable to toggle Wi-Fi
      since Android 10 regardless. The buyer is sent to the system's own settings panel instead.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_WIFI_STATE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE" />
    <!--
      The legacy Bluetooth permission, needed only to read whether the adapter is on, and only up to
      API 30 — from API 31 that call requires no permission at all, so the declaration is capped rather
      than left to look broader than it is on modern phones.
    -->
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.BLUETOOTH"
        android:maxSdkVersion="30" />

    <!--
      Declared as not required, so the app still installs on a handset without one. A phone with no
      microphone is not a phone, but a tablet is a plausible device for a shop to run this on, and the
      microphone screen degrades to saying it cannot record rather than the app being uninstallable.
    -->
    <uses-feature
        android:name="android.hardware.microphone"
        android:required="false" />

    <!--
      Both marked not required, which matters more than it looks. Declaring the CAMERA permission makes
      Play infer android.hardware.camera as required by default, which would hide the app from every
      device without one. This app is for inspecting a handset, and a shop may well run it on a cheap
      tablet; the camera screen degrades to saying it found no cameras rather than the app being
      uninstallable.
    -->
    <uses-feature
        android:name="android.hardware.camera"
        android:required="false" />
    <uses-feature
        android:name="android.hardware.camera.flash"
        android:required="false" />

    <!--
      The same trap as the camera above, and easy to miss. Declaring ACCESS_WIFI_STATE makes Play infer
      android.hardware.wifi as required, and the legacy BLUETOOTH permission implies
      android.hardware.bluetooth; either would quietly hide the app from devices without that radio.
      Both are declared not required, and the radio screen reports honestly when the hardware is absent
      instead of the app being uninstallable.
    -->
    <uses-feature
        android:name="android.hardware.wifi"
        android:required="false" />
    <uses-feature
        android:name="android.hardware.bluetooth"
        android:required="false" />

    <queries>
        <!--
          Makes device-administrator apps visible so their real name can be shown instead of a raw
          package id. On a test device the lock check reported "com.microsoft.office.outlook", which
          is accurate but reads like a fault to anyone who is not a developer; with this it reads
          "Outlook".

          Deliberately scoped to apps declaring a device-admin receiver — exactly the set this check
          reports on — rather than requesting QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES, which Play treats as sensitive and
          which would be wildly disproportionate for resolving a handful of names.
        -->
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.app.action.DEVICE_ADMIN_ENABLED" />
        </intent>

        <!--
          Root manager apps, listed individually so the root check can see whether one is installed.
          A named list rather than QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES: this is the entire set the check asks about,
          and blanket package visibility would be disproportionate and would invite review scrutiny.

          These apps commonly hide themselves, so a negative result here proves nothing on its own.
          It is one of three independent signals, alongside the su binary scan and verified-boot
          state, which is why the check never reports a clean pass on this evidence alone.
        -->
        <package android:name="com.topjohnwu.magisk" />
        <package android:name="io.github.huskydg.magisk" />
        <package android:name="eu.chainfire.supersu" />
        <package android:name="com.noshufou.android.su" />
        <package android:name="com.thirdparty.superuser" />
        <package android:name="com.koushikdutta.superuser" />
        <package android:name="me.weishu.kernelsu" />
    </queries>

    <!--
      No permissions are declared yet, and that is deliberate.
      Every permission gets added only when the check that needs it is built, and is
      requested at the moment of use with the reason shown on screen.
      Never declared: READ_PHONE_STATE (IMEI is unreadable since Android 10 anyway) and
      QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES (a <queries> element is used instead).
    -->

    <application
        android:name=".PhoneProofApplication"
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
        android:fullBackupContent="false"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.PhoneProof">

        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:configChanges="orientation|screenSize|screenLayout|keyboardHidden|uiMode"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.PhoneProof">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>
    </application>

</manifest>
